<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ii Naosuke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ii Naosuke was lord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikone">Hikone</a> domain and, from 1858, great elder (tairō) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>. He signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harris-treaty">Harris Treaty</a> opening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> to Western trade without imperial approval and purged his opponents in the Ansei Purge, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-nariaki">Tokugawa Nariaki</a> of Mito. In 1860 Mito <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> assassinated him outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a>'s Sakurada Gate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ii-naosuke.jpg" alt="Ii Naosuke" width="320" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kanō Eigaku · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ii_Naosuke_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Harris Treaty of 1858, which opened further ports, granted extraterritoriality to foreigners and imposed low fixed tariffs, was concluded without imperial sanction and provoked fierce opposition; its architect, the chief minister <b>Ii Naosuke</b>, was assassinated outside Edo Castle in 1860.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When the court of Emperor Kōmei rebuffed the Treaty of Amity and Commerce, <b>Ii Naosuke</b>, appointed tairō in 1858, signed it anyway and launched the Ansei Purge against over one hundred political enemies.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Rōnin samurai of the Mito and Satsuma domains killed <b>Ii Naosuke</b>, Chief Minister of the Tokugawa shogunate, outside the Sakurada Gate of Edo Castle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-24">24 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ii Naosuke</b>, tairo from 1858, signed the treaty with the United States without imperial approval and purged opponents in the Ansei purge.</blockquote><a href="/state/hikone-domain">Hikone Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Mito men killed the great elder <b>Ii Naosuke</b> in 1860.</blockquote><a href="/state/mito-domain">Mito Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rakuraku-en, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Sakurada-mon, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.28,136.25&amp;year=1844" aria-label="Rakuraku-en on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7998 888 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ii Naosuke"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8448 1158h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8448 1158h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.28,136.25&amp;year=1844">What was here in 1844? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
